How the lifted code is compiled: three exact experiments of sprint 5, session 6. NOT merged: none moved the game's job chain.

1. The capture tracking limit of the hot pieces (RX_HOT_CAPTURE=1000000; the build has 5000): the context is a noalias,
   nocapture pointer and the hot functions use it 10,000-64,000 times, so past the limit clang gives the nocapture up and
   every store to guest memory ends the guest registers' lives.
2. The loop polls' call without the context (-DA64_POLL_CUR: the same poll at the same places, a64_poll_cur()).
3. The lifted functions' calling convention (A64_FAST_CC=1 at lift time, RX_FAST_CC=1 preserve_none / 2 System V): no
   Windows x64 register saves in every lifted function; host calls through stubs (hos_hostcall.c).
Also here: HOS_FP_FTZ (a measurement for the FP tests' ceiling).

Static effect on f_8008fc00 (18,942 instructions): context loads 2,134 -> 328, stores 2,940 -> 824 with 1 + 2.
In the game (castle-move, 3 interleaved rounds, job traces): the main thread's job wait a frame 6.94 / 7.29 (reference;
one disturbed run 8.56), 7.61 / 6.90 / 7.03 (1 + 2), 6.98 / 6.89 / 6.99 (1 + 2 + preserve_none), 6.86 / 6.91 / 7.25
(1 + 2 + System V); fps 81.6 / 82.2 / 84.3 / 82.8. Picture and speed right in every variant. The job chain is not held by
this overhead. docs/tickets/SPRINT-5.md, session 6.

